Arrow matrix disaster An arrow matrix $A$ has the pattern: nonzeros only on the diagonal and in the first row and column. If you eliminate in natural order $(1, 2, \ldots, n)$, how much fill-in occurs?    Nested dissection on a 2D grid A $3 	imes 3$ grid of unknowns (9 nodes, 12 edges) forms the 2D Poisson stiffness matrix. Describe one level of nested dissection and identify the separator.    	ext{Grid nodes: } egin{bmatrix}1&2&3\4&5&6\7&8&9\end{bmatrix} Label the 9 unknowns in the grid. 	ext{Separator: column 2} = \{2, 5, 8\} Removing nodes 2, 5, 8 (the middle column) splits the grid into left partition $\{1, 4, 7\}$ and right partition $\{3, 6, 9\}$. The separator has size $\sqrt{n} = 3$. 	ext{Reorder: } [1,4,7,\; 3,6,9,\; 2,5,8] = [	ext{left}, 	ext{right}, 	ext{separator}]$ Eliminate the two independent subproblems first (left and right blocks create no cross-fill), then eliminate the separator last. 	ext{Fill-in: only within each subproblem block and in the separator — } O(n \log n) 	ext{ total} The key property: nodes in the left partition never directly interact with nodes in the right partition (only through the separator). This limits cross-fill-in to the separator column block. Recursing on the subproblems gives the $O(n \log n)$ bound. Direct vs iterative: when each wins Compare the total work for solving a 2D Poisson problem with $N = n^2$ unknowns using (a) sparse direct with nested dissection, (b) CG without preconditioning, (c) CG + AMG. Include both setup and solve costs.    	ext{Direct (ND): setup } O(N^{3/2}), 	ext{ per solve } O(N\log N) Factoring with ND: $O(N^{3/2})$ flops. Each subsequent solve via triangular back-substitution: $O(N \log N)$ flops (because $L$ and $U$ have $O(N \log N)$ non-zeros after ND). 	ext{CG (no precond): } O(\sqrt{\kappa}) 	ext{ iters, each } O(N) \Rightarrow O(N^{3/2}) 	ext{ total} For 2D Poisson: $\kappa = O(n^2) = O(N)$, so CG needs $O(\sqrt{N})$ iterations, each costing $O(N)$ for the sparse matvec. 	ext{CG+AMG: } O(1) 	ext{ iters (bounded), each } O(N) \Rightarrow O(N) 	ext{ total} AMG achieves $\kappa(M^{-1}A) = O(1)$ — a bounded constant independent of $N$. So CG+AMG needs a fixed number of iterations regardless of $N$. Each iteration costs $O(N)$ (AMG V-cycle). 	ext{Winner: 1 RHS} \Rightarrow 	ext{CG+AMG}; \quad k 	ext{ RHS} \Rightarrow 	ext{Direct if } k > N^{1/2}/\log N$ For many right-hand sides, the direct solver's cheap per-solve cost ($O(N \log N)$) wins over AMG's setup cost. For a single RHS with large $N$, AMG wins. The crossover depends on problem size and number of RHS. Elimination tree depth: path vs nested dissection For a tridiagonal (path graph) matrix with natural ordering, describe the elimination tree and its depth. Then explain how nested dissection changes the tree structure and what that means for parallel factorization.    T(A)_{	ext{natural}}: n-1 	o n-2 	o \cdots 	o 1 \quad (	ext{depth} = n-1) Natural order: eliminating column $i$ creates a fill entry in column $i+1$ (the first nonzero to the right). So column $i+1$ is the parent of column $i$. The result is a chain — depth $n-1$. Every column depends on the previous one: no parallelism at all. 	ext{Critical path: depth}(T) \Rightarrow 	ext{minimum sequential steps} The depth of the elimination tree equals the minimum number of sequential factorization steps, regardless of how many processors are available. Depth $n-1$ means the factorization is inherently sequential for a tridiagonal with natural ordering — adding processors does not help. T(A)_{	ext{ND}}: 	ext{balanced binary tree, depth} = O(\log n) Nested dissection ordering: the separator node is numbered last (highest index). Each of the two sub-problems is itself ND-ordered, recursively. The elimination tree becomes a balanced binary tree with depth $O(\log n)$. All nodes at the same depth in the tree are independent and can be computed in parallel. 	ext{Parallel speedup} = O\!\left( rac{n}{\log n}ight) 	ext{ on }  rac{n}{\log n} 	ext{ processors} With $p = n/\log n$ processors and depth $O(\log n)$, Brent's theorem gives total time $O(\log n)$, giving speedup $O(n/\log n)$. For large $n$ this is near-optimal parallel efficiency. This is precisely why MUMPS and other parallel sparse solvers use ND ordering. Tridiagonal: zero fill-in under natural order Prove that a tridiagonal $n 	imes n$ matrix $A$ produces zero fill-in when eliminated in natural order $(1, 2, \ldots, n)$.    	ext{Eliminate column 1: only } a_{21} 
eq 0 	ext{ below diagonal} Column 1 of a tridiagonal has nonzeros only at positions $(1,1)$ and $(2,1)$. The Schur complement update modifies only the $(2,2)$ entry (already nonzero) — no new nonzero entries are created. 	ext{After eliminating column 1: row 2 is unchanged except } a_{22} The only row that gets modified is row 2 (the only row with a nonzero in column 1 below the diagonal). Row 2 already has nonzeros at columns 2 and 3 — no new nonzeros appear. 	ext{Same argument at every step } i: 	ext{ column } i 	ext{ has one subdiagonal entry at row } i+1 By induction: when we reach column $i$, it has already been partially updated but remains tridiagonal in its structure. There is exactly one nonzero below $(i,i)$, at row $i+1$. The update only touches row $i+1$ at columns $i+1$ and $i+2$ — both already occupied. 	ext{Fill-in} = 0 \Rightarrow L, U 	ext{ are bidiagonal} Conclusion: the LU factors of a tridiagonal are bidiagonal (lower and upper). This is the reason 1D finite difference problems are trivially solved by direct methods — no fill-in, $O(n)$ factorization, exactly like the original matrix. Arrow matrix fill-in: natural vs reverse order An $n 	imes n$ arrow matrix $A$ has nonzeros only on the diagonal and in the first row and column (i.e., $a_{1j} 
eq 0$ and $a_{i1} 
eq 0$ for all $i, j$, and $a_{ij} = 0$ for $i,j \geq 2, i 
eq j$). Compute the fill-in under (a) natural order and (b) reverse order $(n, n-1, \ldots, 1)$.    A =  egin{bmatrix} * & * & * & * \ * & * & & \ * & & * & \ * & & & * \end{bmatrix} \quad (	ext{pattern, } n=4) The arrow (or "arrowhead") matrix: dense first row and column, diagonal elsewhere. Variables $2, \ldots, n$ are each connected only to variable 1 — they are "leaves" attached to the hub at node 1. 	ext{Natural order: eliminate column 1 first} Eliminating column 1: rows $2, 3, \ldots, n$ all have a nonzero in column 1. After the elimination, every pair of rows $(i, j)$ with $i,j \geq 2$ gets a new entry at column $j$ if row $j$ was involved. The entire $(2:n) 	imes (2:n)$ block fills in completely. 	ext{Fill-in (natural)} = (n-1)^2 - (n-1) = (n-1)(n-2) = O(n^2) After eliminating column 1, the remaining $(n-1) 	imes (n-1)$ submatrix is dense — all entries filled in. From $n-1$ nonzeros, we created $(n-1)^2$ nonzeros. This is the worst case for direct methods without reordering. 	ext{Reverse order: eliminate columns } n, n-1, \ldots, 2 	ext{ first} Column $n$ has nonzeros only at $(n,n)$ and $(1,n)$. The only row below the diagonal in column $n$ is... none (column $n$ has no subdiagonal entries). Wait — the arrowhead has nonzeros in column 1 below the diagonal, but in columns $2, \ldots, n$ only the first row and diagonal are nonzero. So eliminating column $k \geq 2$ first: there is no nonzero below the diagonal, so no elimination step occurs at all. 	ext{Fill-in (reverse)} = 0 \quad 	ext{(eliminating leaves creates nothing)} Leaves $n, n-1, \ldots, 2$ each have one above-diagonal entry (in row 1) and one diagonal entry. No subdiagonal entries to create fill. Eliminating them in reverse order: zero fill-in until the very last step. Column 1 is eliminated last and it is already alone — zero fill-in for the whole factorization. 	ext{Lesson: reordering transforms } O(n^2) 	o O(n) 	ext{ fill-in on the same matrix} The matrix values did not change — only the variable ordering changed. The graph interpretation: natural order eliminates the hub (node 1) first, forcing all leaves to connect to each other (complete graph). Reverse order eliminates leaves first, which have no mutual connections — the hub is eliminated last with nothing left.

