Every game engine and physics simulator runs the same pipeline every frame: sum up all the forces on a body, divide by mass to get acceleration, integrate to get velocity, integrate again to get position. This lesson builds that pipeline from scratch — you'll see why cloth sags, why planets orbit, and how your mouse click turns into a 3D ray. Semi-implicit vs explicit Euler **Explicit:** `v_new = v + a*dt`, `p_new = p + v*dt` (uses OLD velocity for position — unstable for oscillators). **Semi-implicit:** `v_new = v + a*dt`, `p_new = p + v_new*dt` (uses NEW velocity for position — stable for springs and gravity). For springs with explicit Euler, energy grows without bound. Semi-implicit keeps it bounded. Hooke's Law spring force `F = −k × (|AB| − L₀) × normalize(AB)` where k is stiffness, L₀ is rest length. Add damping: `F_damp = −c × dot(v_rel, normalize(AB)) × normalize(AB)` where `v_rel = v_B − v_A`. Apply equal and opposite: push on A, pull on B (Newton's third law). Mouse-to-ray mapping ```js
// Normalize mouse to [-1,+1] range
const rect = renderer.domElement.getBoundingClientRect()
const mouse = new THREE.Vector2(
  (event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width * 2 - 1,
 -(event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height * 2 + 1
)
raycaster.setFromCamera(mouse, camera)
const hits = raycaster.intersectObjects(pickable)
if (hits.length > 0) { const { point, face } = hits[0] }
```

