documents.applications:
Every 3D engine — Unreal, Unity, Three.js, Blender — stores objects the same way. Shape and surface are kept separate on purpose. The shape (geometry) is a mesh of triangles. The surface (material) is a set of rules for how light bounces off it. Combining them gives you a renderable object that can be repositioned, rotated, and scaled independently.

This separation is powerful: you can give 10 pins the same geometry (one copy of the shape in GPU memory) with 10 different positions, or swap a metallic material for a rubber one without touching the shape at all. Learning this pattern in Three.js gives you a mental model that transfers directly to every other 3D tool you will ever use. Lesson 1 of Sim-3: 3D Bowling **Previous:** Sim-2 (2D function plotter in Canvas)
**This lesson:** Three.js scene graph — Geometry + Material = Mesh, lighting models, multiple mesh positions.
**Next (L02):** Pin Formation — THREE.Vector3, Groups, placing 10 pins in the regulation diamond pattern. The Three-Step Recipe ```js
// Step 1 — shape (only vertices, no colour)
const geo = new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1)

// Step 2 — surface (only appearance, no shape)
const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x44aaff })

// Step 3 — combine + register in the world
const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geo, mat)
scene.add(mesh)
```

Geometry and Material are independent objects. Many Meshes can share one Geometry (one GPU upload, many positions) or one Material (shared surface, different shapes). Y-Up Coordinate System Three.js uses a right-handed, Y-up coordinate system:

• **+X** → right
• **+Y** → up (toward the ceiling)
• **+Z** → toward the camera

A BoxGeometry centred at origin has its top face at `y = height / 2`. To stack B on top of A:

```js
b.position.y = A_HEIGHT / 2 + B_HEIGHT / 2
```

Always think in centre-points, not edges. `position.set()` always sets the centre of the object. Sharing Geometry is Free Performance The left gutter and right gutter have identical shapes. Pass the same Geometry object to both Mesh constructors:

```js
const gutterGeo = new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.25, 0.06, 18.3)
const leftGutter  = new THREE.Mesh(gutterGeo, mat)
const rightGutter = new THREE.Mesh(gutterGeo, mat)
```

The vertex data is uploaded to the GPU exactly once. Each Mesh stores only its own transform (position, rotation, scale). With hundreds of identical objects this becomes **instancing** — the technique games use to render thousands of trees or bricks. Don't Create Meshes in update() Creating a `THREE.Mesh` allocates CPU memory, uploads vertex data to the GPU, and can trigger garbage collection. Do all object creation in `init()`. In `update()`, only read and modify existing meshes:

```js
// correct
function init()      { ballMesh = new THREE.Mesh(geo, mat); scene.add(ballMesh) }
function update(dt)  { ballMesh.position.z -= ROLL_SPEED * dt }

// wrong — re-creates the mesh 60 times per second
function update(dt)  { scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(geo, mat)) }
```

