In every physics engine — Unity, Unreal, Bullet, Havok — every collision resolves the same way: decompose the relative velocity into the component along the contact normal (the push) and the component perpendicular to it (the friction/spin). Both decompositions come from exactly two operations: the dot product and the cross product.

These two operations also appear in every other domain of simulation: the dot product decides whether a character is facing the player (AI), whether a face polygon is visible (graphics culling), and how much force a rope exerts along its length (constraints). The cross product computes surface normals for lighting, torque for rigid bodies, and the axis of rotation for any joint. Learning them in a concrete physics context makes abstract linear algebra tangible. L03 Challenge Answer — Closest Pin ```js
const tempVec = new THREE.Vector3(); // module scope, reused each frame

// In update():
let closestDist = Infinity;
let closestPin  = null;

pinMeshes.forEach(pin => {
  pin.getWorldPosition(tempVec);
  const dist = tempVec.distanceTo(ballMesh.position);
  if (dist < closestDist) { closestDist = dist; closestPin = pin; }
});

pinMeshes.forEach(pin => { pin.material.color.setHex(PIN_WHITE_HEX); });
if (closestPin && closestDist < HIGHLIGHT_DIST) {
  closestPin.material.color.setHex(PIN_CLOSE_HEX);
}
```

`getWorldPosition()` is needed because pins are children of `pinGroup` — their `position` property is local to the group, not world space. Dot & Cross in Three.js ```js
const a = new THREE.Vector3(1, 0, 0);
const b = new THREE.Vector3(0, 0, -1);

// Dot product — returns a NUMBER (scalar)
const alignment = a.dot(b);        // = 0 (perpendicular)

// Cross product — .cross() modifies IN PLACE → always .clone() first
const perp = a.clone().cross(b);   // = (0, 1, 0) by right-hand rule

// Scalar projection of a onto b (how much of a acts along b direction)
const proj = a.dot(b.clone().normalize());

// Vector projection
const bUnit = b.clone().normalize();
const vecProj = bUnit.clone().multiplyScalar(a.dot(bUnit));
``` Right-Hand Rule `a × b` direction: point fingers of right hand along `a`, curl toward `b` — thumb points in the direction of the result.

Standard axis checks:
• `X × Y = Z`  (right × up = forward)
• `Y × Z = X`  (up × forward = right)
• `Z × X = Y`  (forward × right = up)

Order matters: `a × b = −(b × a)`. Swapping the arguments flips the direction.

