Every interactive simulation has a control layer above its physics layer. In professional physics engines this is called a "game loop" or "simulation manager." It implements a finite state machine: a set of named phases, a variable tracking the current phase, and transition rules.

The same pattern appears at every scale. A bowling game has ROLLING → SETTLING → SCORING states. A character controller has IDLE → RUNNING → JUMPING → FALLING states. A network protocol has CONNECTING → HANDSHAKING → OPEN → CLOSING states. A CI pipeline has QUEUED → BUILDING → TESTING → DEPLOYING states. The implementation is always the same: one variable, one switch, explicit transitions. Learning to write a state machine in a concrete physics context gives you the template for orchestrating any time-varying system. L06 Challenge Answer — Tuned Impulse Constants ```js
// Values that give a convincing 1-3 pocket scatter:
const TRANSFER_FRACTION = 0.85;  // slightly energetic — pins fly fast enough to chain
const SCATTER_FRACTION  = 0.45;  // enough sideways motion for off-centre hits
const PIN_PIN_TRANSFER  = 0.65;  // secondary hits stay energetic
const PIN_PIN_SCATTER   = 0.30;  // moderate secondary spread
const FLOOR_FRICTION    = 0.90;  // pins decelerate in ~1 second
```

If right-side pins (6, 9, 10) were not falling: increase `SCATTER_FRACTION` or `PIN_PIN_SCATTER`. If pins stopped before reaching the back row: reduce `FLOOR_FRICTION` toward 0.94–0.96. State Machine Pattern ```js
const STATE_ROLLING  = 'ROLLING';
const STATE_SETTLING = 'SETTLING';
const STATE_SCORING  = 'SCORING';
const STATE_WAITING  = 'WAITING';

let gameState  = STATE_ROLLING;
let stateTimer = 0;

function update(deltaTime) {
  stateTimer += deltaTime;

  if (gameState === STATE_ROLLING) {
    // advance ball, run collision detection
    if (ball.z < PIN_DECK_Z - 0.5) {
      gameState = STATE_SETTLING; stateTimer = 0;
    }
  }
  else if (gameState === STATE_SETTLING) {
    if (allPinsSettled() || stateTimer > MAX_SETTLE_TIME) {
      gameState = STATE_SCORING; stateTimer = 0;
      countPinsDown();
    }
  }
  else if (gameState === STATE_SCORING) {
    if (stateTimer > SCORING_DURATION) {
      gameState = STATE_WAITING; stateTimer = 0;
    }
  }
  else if (gameState === STATE_WAITING) {
    if (stateTimer > WAITING_DURATION) {
      resetForNextThrow();
      gameState = STATE_ROLLING; stateTimer = 0;
    }
  }

  advancePinPhysics(deltaTime);  // runs every frame regardless of state
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}
``` userData.homeLocalPos Pins start in group-local positions set during scene setup. Once physics moves them, those local positions change permanently. Store the original position at creation time: `pin.userData.homeLocalPos = new THREE.Vector3(localX, localY, localZ)`. Between-frame reset then calls `pin.position.copy(pin.userData.homeLocalPos)` to restore every pin to its starting spot.

documents.content:
All the physics pieces are built. How does the simulation know when a frame starts, when it ends, when to score, and when to reset? Every interactive simulation has a control layer above its physics layer. In professional physics engines this is called a "game loop" or "simulation manager." It implements a finite state machine: a set of named phases, a variable tracking the current phase, and transition rules.

The same pattern appears at every scale. A bowling game has ROLLING → SETTLING → SCORING states. A character controller has IDLE → RUNNING → JUMPING → FALLING states. A network protocol has CONNECTING → HANDSHAKING → OPEN → CLOSING states. A CI pipeline has QUEUED → BUILDING → TESTING → DEPLOYING states. The implementation is always the same: one variable, one switch, explicit transitions. Learning to write a state machine in a concrete physics context gives you the template for orchestrating any time-varying system. **The state variable gates logic.** A single string (or integer) variable called `gameState` controls which code runs each frame. The ball only moves when `gameState === ROLLING`. Collision detection only runs in rolling states. Score display only runs in `SCORING`. This prevents actions from "leaking" into the wrong phase and makes the system's behaviour predictable. **Transitions are assignments.** A transition is just `gameState = NEXT_STATE` — nothing more. What makes it a transition rather than a plain assignment is *where* it appears: inside a conditional that tests the transition condition. `if (ball.z < PIN_DECK_Z) { gameState = SETTLING; stateTimer = 0; }` is a complete transition: condition, state change, timer reset. **Between-throw vs between-frame reset.** A two-throw frame requires two kinds of reset. Between throws: only the ball returns to the foul line. Downed pins stay down and are marked `pin.userData.isDown = true` so throw 2 skips them in collision detection. Between frames: everything resets — pins return to `pin.userData.homeLocalPos`, all physics state clears, `isDown` flags reset. **Settle detection.** After the ball passes the pins, the simulation must wait for all motion to stop before scoring. A timer-based settle (`wait 1.5 s`) is simple but may cut off slow pins. A motion-based settle checks `pin.userData.vel.lengthSq() < threshold` and `fallAngle >= MAX - epsilon` for every pin. Combining both (`motion check OR timeout`) is robust: fast settle when possible, always exits. L06 Challenge Answer — Tuned Impulse Constants ```js
// Values that give a convincing 1-3 pocket scatter:
const TRANSFER_FRACTION = 0.85;  // slightly energetic — pins fly fast enough to chain
const SCATTER_FRACTION  = 0.45;  // enough sideways motion for off-centre hits
const PIN_PIN_TRANSFER  = 0.65;  // secondary hits stay energetic
const PIN_PIN_SCATTER   = 0.30;  // moderate secondary spread
const FLOOR_FRICTION    = 0.90;  // pins decelerate in ~1 second
```

If right-side pins (6, 9, 10) were not falling: increase `SCATTER_FRACTION` or `PIN_PIN_SCATTER`. If pins stopped before reaching the back row: reduce `FLOOR_FRICTION` toward 0.94–0.96. State Machine Pattern ```js
const STATE_ROLLING  = 'ROLLING';
const STATE_SETTLING = 'SETTLING';
const STATE_SCORING  = 'SCORING';
